.TH CTI-ALERT 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-alert \- tell the operator that a fleet unit failed, is holding, or needs a person
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti-alert
.B \-\-unit
.I UNIT
.RB [ \-\-kind
.IR KIND ]
.RB [ \-\-reason
.IR TEXT ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
.B cti-alert
.RI [ options ]
.I UNIT
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-alert
.B \-\-unit
.I UNIT
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti-alert
reports on one systemd unit to the fleet operator, through two channels:
.TP
.B The message board
One line posted with
.IR $FLEET_CODE/bin/fleet\-board " " post " " @systemd " " @operator ,
which needs no network and is read by the orchestrator on its next
heartbeat. Posted first, because it is the channel most likely to survive
whatever broke.
.TP
.B Email
One message through Microsoft Graph, sent as
.B GRAPH_MAILBOX
to
.B FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
(or
.B DIGEST_TO
when that is empty). It quotes systemd's
.BR Result ,
.B ExecMainStatus
and
.B NRestarts
for the unit, the last 40 lines of its journal, and the commands to run
next. Marked high importance only for a failure of a unit whose name
contains
.B digest
or
.BR weekly ,
because a missing digest looks exactly like a quiet day.
.PP
It is run by:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cti\-agent\-alert@.service ,
started by
.B OnFailure=
on every fleet service as
.BR "cti\-alert \-\-unit %i" .
That unit has no
.B OnFailure=
of its own, so a failing alert cannot loop.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR run\-checkin (1)
with
.B \-\-kind HOLD
when
.BR cti\-budget (1)
stops the heartbeat, and the orchestrator itself, for which this is the only
mail it is allowed to send
.RB ( "\-\-kind ESCALATION" ).
.IP \(bu 2
.BR run\-appscan (1)
with
.B \-\-kind HOLD
when a scanner credential failed.
.IP \(bu 2
The operator, by hand, to test the alert path.
.PP
The headline word decides how the message looks:
.TP
.B FAILED
Red banner, an
.B ERROR
line on the board. The default. A
.B FAILED
alert for a unit that is visibly healthy (exit status
.B 0
and result
.BR success ,
empty or unknown) is rewritten to
.B TEST
before anything is sent, so a hand-run test cannot open an incident.
.TP
.B HOLD
Amber banner, an
.B INFO
line on the board, never high importance: the lane stopped itself on
purpose.
.TP
.B TEST
Green banner, an
.B INFO
line on the board. Inferred as above, or given explicitly.
.TP
.B ESCALATION
Navy banner: the orchestrator needs a person and nothing is broken. On the
board it is an
.B INFO
"escalated to the operator" line carrying the reason.
.PP
Any other word is uppercased and shown in the subject, and is otherwise
treated as
.BR FAILED ,
so a mistyped kind errs towards alarm.
.PP
The subject is
.IR "[CTI FLEET KIND] UNIT on HOST" .
.PP
.B cti-alert
has no recipient option and cannot be pointed anywhere but the operator
address in
.IR fleet.env .
It does not go through
.BR cti\-mailer (1)
or the
.B VM_ALLOW_TO
gate: mail to the operator is pre-approved. It never restarts, stops or
changes the unit it reports on.
.SH OPTIONS
Go's flag syntax: each option may also be written with a single dash
.RB ( \-unit ),
and a value may follow
.B =
or a space.
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " UNIT"
The unit to report on, for example
.BR cti\-agent\-digest.service .
Required; when omitted, the first argument after the options is used
instead. With neither, nothing is sent and the exit status is 2.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-kind " KIND"
The headline word:
.BR FAILED ,
.BR HOLD ,
.B ESCALATION
or
.BR TEST ,
case-insensitive. See DESCRIPTION.
Default:\~\fBFAILED\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-reason " TEXT"
One line shown above the systemd detail, in the email and in a
.B HOLD
board line. When a
.B FAILED
alert is turned into
.B TEST
and no reason was given, a standard "manual test of the alert path" reason
is supplied.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the subject, the board line that would be posted, and the plain-text
body. Sends no email and posts nothing to the board.
Default:\~off.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is loaded at start from
.B FLEET_ENV
(else
.IR $FLEET_HOME/fleet.env ,
else
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent/fleet.env );
variables already in the environment win.
.TP
.BR TENANT_ID ", " CLIENT_ID ", " CLIENT_SECRET
The Entra app registration used to send.
.I fleet.env
section 1.
.TP
.B GRAPH_MAILBOX
The mailbox the alert is sent as. Section 1.
.TP
.B FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
Recipients, comma-separated. A person, not a distribution list.
Section 4.
.TP
.B DIGEST_TO
Used as the recipient list only when
.B FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
is empty. Section 4.
.TP
.B FLEET_CODE
Where
.B bin/fleet\-board
is found. No
.B fleet\-board
there means no board post.
Default:\~\fI/opt/cti\-agent\fR.
.TP
.BR FLEET_ENV ", " FLEET_HOME
Locate
.IR fleet.env .
.PP
If any of the Graph variables or both recipient variables are empty, the
email is not sent; the reason, and the full text of the alert, go to
standard error (the journal), and the board post still happens.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env
Credentials and the operator address.
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/bin/fleet\-board
The board the alert is posted to.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/cti\-agent\-alert@.service
The template unit that runs it, as
.BR ctiagent ,
with
.B SupplementaryGroups=systemd\-journal
so it can read the failed unit's journal. Run by hand without that group,
the journal section reads
.IR "(journal unavailable: ...)" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Always, once a unit name is known: whether or not the board post or the
email worked. A failed send is reported on standard error, not in the exit
status, so the alert unit is never itself marked failed and
.B systemctl \-\-failed
still names the unit that really broke.
.TP
.B 2
No unit was given, or an option was not recognised.
.B \-\-help
prints the usage and exits 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Test the alert path end to end (the unit is healthy, so this arrives as a
green TEST):
.PP
.EX
sudo systemctl start cti\-agent\-alert@cti\-agent\-digest.service.service
.EE
.PP
See what would be sent, without mailing (this still posts to the board):
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-alert \-\-unit cti\-agent\-digest.service \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.PP
Report a deliberate hold:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-alert \-\-unit cti\-agent\-checkin.service \e
    \-\-kind HOLD \-\-reason "paused for maintenance on host\-a"
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-budget (1),
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR run\-appscan (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 15; fleet\-kit/README.md, "Failure alerting".
